/-! # Properness of affine morphisms tested by formal arcs

Source: the agent formulation accompanying Mumford--Fogarty--Kirwan,
Geometric Invariant Theory, Chapter 2, Section 1, proof of Theorem 2.1;
its recorded proof uses Stacks Project, Tag 0GWX, and completion of curve local rings.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-affine-properness-formal-arc-test).

The test rings are `PowerSeries k` and `LaurentSeries k` over the given field `k`.
The canonical inclusion is the algebra map from power series to Laurent series.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- An affine finite-type morphism over an algebraically closed field is proper exactly
when every compatible formal-arc square over that field has a unique lift.
Source: the agent formulation attached to Mumford--Fogarty--Kirwan, GIT, Chapter 2,
Section 1, proof of Theorem 2.1; the local proof below follows Stacks Tag 0GWX.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-affine-properness-formal-arc-test).

The structure morphisms `sX` and `sZ` specify the given `k`-schemes. Equations with
`Spec.map` of coefficient maps say exactly that `a`, `b` and the lift are `k`-morphisms.
No reducedness or characteristic assumption is imposed.

Proof (the six steps of the DAG node):
1. By lem-weil-pbc-power-series-field-cohen-regular-complete for d = 1, k[[t]] is
   a Noetherian local domain with maximal ideal (t), where t is nonzero. It is a DVR
   by def-sc-discrete-valuation-ring. The unit-times-uniformizer factorization of
   lem-weil-pbc-dvr-unit-times-uniformizer-power identifies its fraction field with
   k((t)). If f is proper, lemma-nagata-proper-separated-valuation-extension gives
   the unique lift. Its composite with f makes it a k-morphism.
2. Conversely, f is quasi-compact because affine schemes are quasi-compact and
   principal opens pull back to principal opens (lem-weil-pbc-affine-scheme-qcqs).
   Generators over k also generate over the target affine coordinate ring. Use
   lem-sfb-properness-normal-curve-local-test. For its normal curve C and closed
   point c, R = O_(C,c) is a Noetherian normal one-dimensional local domain by
   pqf-lem-locnoeth-lft, hence a DVR by lem-sc-normal-dim1-dvr. Its residue field
   is finite over k by lem-kun-closed-point-field, hence k by algebraic closedness.
3. Choose a uniformizer pi. The completion H of R is a DVR by
   lem-weil-pbc-completion-of-dvr-is-dvr. Its maximal ideal is generated by pi and
   its residue field is k (lem-weil-pbc-local-completion-local-ring). It is complete
   by lem-weil-pbc-adic-completion-mod-ideal. Uniformizer factorization shows that
   a DVR has dimension one and is regular (def-weil-regular-scheme). Then
   lem-trf-regular-complete-coefficient-coordinates identifies H with k[[t]] as a
   k-algebra, taking pi to t.
4. Uniformizer factorization shows R injects into H, inducing Frac(R) into k((t)).
   Extend the generic curve map to k((t)) and restrict the base map to Spec H.
   Their compatibility gives a formal-arc square. The hypothesis gives a lift
   Spec H to X (def-weil-affine-spectrum).
5. Inside k((t)), Frac(R) intersect H equals R: a fraction is a unit times an
   integral power of pi; a negative power cannot be integral in H, since a unit
   cannot belong to its maximal ideal (lem-weil-pbc-dvr-unit-times-uniformizer-power).
   The generic and completed coordinate maps agree in k((t)), so every image of
   the affine coordinate ring of X lies in R. This constructs the lift Spec R to X
   and preserves the base map by injectivity (def-weil-affine-spectrum).
6. Two such lifts agree after the injection R into Frac(R), hence agree on coordinate
   rings and as scheme morphisms (def-weil-affine-spectrum). The normal-curve local
   test proves f proper. The argument never replaces X or Z by its reduction.
-/
theorem isProper_iff_affine_formal_arc_test
    (k : Type u) [Field k] [IsAlgClosed k]
    {X Z : Scheme.{u}} [IsAffine X] [IsAffine Z]
    (sX : X ⟶ Spec (.of k)) (sZ : Z ⟶ Spec (.of k))
    [LocallyOfFiniteType sX] [QuasiCompact sX]
    [LocallyOfFiniteType sZ] [QuasiCompact sZ]
    (f : X ⟶ Z) (hf : f ≫ sZ = sX) :
    IsProper f ↔
      ∀ (a : Spec (.of (LaurentSeries k)) ⟶ X)
        (b : Spec (.of (PowerSeries k)) ⟶ Z),
        a ≫ sX = Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap k (LaurentSeries k))) →
        b ≫ sZ = Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap k (PowerSeries k))) →
        a ≫ f = Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom
          (algebraMap (PowerSeries k) (LaurentSeries k))) ≫ b →
        ∃! lift : Spec (.of (PowerSeries k)) ⟶ X,
          lift ≫ sX = Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap k (PowerSeries k))) ∧
          Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom
            (algebraMap (PowerSeries k) (LaurentSeries k))) ≫ lift = a ∧
          lift ≫ f = b := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
